# Chroma library, modern Earth: TITLE MOMENTS of careers (titles volume 2), an everyday pair per career title that only its holder meets (holds:).
# Syntax: header of build.py. Rules: library-spec.md. Brief: drafts/titles2/MOMENTS-BRIEF.md. Seeds: Emren's "Modern Earth: 100 More Titles" (each pair's first moment grows from the title's turning point).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- laboratory technician (T2-001): calls WB (cycle s1) / calls URG (cycle s2)

== a culture that smells wrong at closing time
tier: everyday
at: employer
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 18-67
calls: WB
holds: laboratory technician
tone: trouble
life: work
horizon: moment
roles: boss, colleague, mentor
earth: at closing time one culture in the incubator smells different, and tomorrow's run depends on whether you speak up
times: Most laboratory technicians, about once a year; contamination is a routine hazard of culture work, and roughly 1 in 300 US workers does lab bench work (estimate from BLS)
gap: 0.8-1
scene: At closing time {N} notices that one of the cultures smells different. Tomorrow's run now depends on whether {N} speaks up.
scene W: The protocol says any suspected contamination is logged and reported before the next run, whoever made the batch. The logbook lies open on the bench.
scene U: The smell is faintly sweet, not the usual warm yeasty note. {N} holds the flask up to the light and starts listing what could cause it.
scene B: Speaking up cancels tomorrow's run, and {boss} will want to know whose batch it was. {N} prepared that batch.
scene R: {Ns} coat is on and the last bus goes in ten minutes, but the smell will not let {N} go.
scene G: After two years at this bench {N} knows how a healthy culture smells, the way a farmer knows a sick cow at a glance. This one is wrong.
- W | log the suspect flask and report it to the supervisor on the way out | v: conformity, security | chance: 88
- U | stay late, plate a sample and look at it under the microscope | v: achievement | self_control: + | chance: 60
- B | come in at six and remake the batch yourself before anyone asks | v: achievement, security | self_control: + | chance: 85
- R | trust your nose and pull the flask out of the run on the spot | v: self-direction | chance: 90
- G | ring your old trainer at home and describe the smell | v: tradition | chance: 70
- W>U | work through the contamination checklist line by line, noting what you learn | v: achievement | mark: learned a skill | chance: 75
- U>B | check the prep log to see whose batch it was, and only then speak | v: power, security | chance: 95
- B>R | slip out for your bus and let the morning shift find it | v: hedonism | closed: approval: leaving a suspected contamination unreported; backfire: the run fails and the log shows who was last in | mark: hid a wrong | self_control: - | chance: 80
- R>G | stay on and split the healthy flasks off by hand, to save what is still alive | v: security | chance: 65
- G>W | set the flask aside with a note for the morning, as the old hands do | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 65
works: The bad flask is caught in time, and the next day's run goes ahead clean.
works: {boss} looks at the plate in the morning, nods, and thanks {N} for the catch.
fails: The run fails at noon the next day, and a week's work goes into the autoclave.
fails: The culture turns out to be fine, and {N} has held up the whole lab for nothing.

== the same two hundred samples a day
tier: everyday
at: employer
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 18-67
calls: URG
holds: laboratory technician
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: months
roles: boss, colleague
earth: months into the job, every day is the same run of samples, and the work has gone dull
times: Most laboratory technicians, in dull stretches most years; routine testing is the bulk of lab work (estimate)
gap: 0.8-1
scene: Eight months in, {N} runs the same two hundred samples every day and could do the whole protocol asleep.
scene W: The routine is the point: every sample handled the same way, so every result means the same thing. The days are still long.
scene U: {N} has noticed that one step of the protocol wastes twenty minutes a day, and nobody has ever asked why it is there.
scene B: {colleague} was moved onto the research team after a year. {N} has been here eight months and is still pipetting the same racks.
scene R: Pipette, spin, label, repeat. By three in the afternoon {N} wants to fling the rack aside and walk out into the sun.
scene G: Under the microscope the cells are never quite the same twice, even when the work is. {N} watches them divide while the centrifuge hums.
- W | keep to the protocol to the letter and let the steady days pay the rent | v: security, conformity | self_control: + | chance: 90
- U | skip the double-check on the samples that always come back normal | v: achievement | self_control: - | closed: approval: skipping a required check; backfire: an abnormal sample slips through | chance: 85
- B | take every overtime shift going, and save it all for a deposit | v: achievement, security | habit | self_control: + | chance: 85
- R | ring in sick on the first warm Friday and go to the coast | v: hedonism | habit | self_control: - | closed: approval: a sick day that is not; backfire: a colleague sees the photographs | chance: 92
- G | take the work one rack at a time, and stop fighting the sameness | v: tradition (acceptance) | self_control: + | chance: 75
- W>B | sign up for the evening certificate that lifts your pay grade | v: achievement | door | binds | grants: professional registration | chance: 60
- U>R | learn to run the new sequencer, so the job stops being the same every day | v: stimulation, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 70
- B>G | bargain for a four-day week and spend the fifth day in the garden | v: self-direction, hedonism | chance: 40
- R>W | tell the team meeting the rota is unfair, and say how to fix it | v: universalism | chance: 40
- G>U | learn from the oldest technician the tricks the manuals leave out | v: tradition, achievement | door | mark: learned a skill | chance: 82
works: The weeks find a better shape, and {N} goes home in the evenings with something left over.
works: {boss} notices, and by spring the work has a part in it that is {Ns} own.
fails: Nothing changes, and by Friday the racks look exactly the way they did on Monday.
fails: {boss} hears about it before {N} is ready, and the conversation goes badly.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- book editor (T2-012): calls BR (cycle s3) / calls WUG (cycle s1)

== the cleanest sentence in the manuscript
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 21-75
calls: BR
holds: book editor
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: week
roles: an author, colleague
earth: deep in an edit, the cleanest sentence in the manuscript is also the one that sounds least like its author
times: Most book editors, a few times a year; smoothing a writer's voice away is a known risk of close editing (estimate)
gap: 0-0.5
scene: Deep in the edit, {N} reaches the cleanest sentence in the whole manuscript, and it is also the one that sounds least like its author.
scene W: The house style asks for clear, correct sentences, and this one is flawless. {N} still feels it belongs to someone else.
scene U: {N} reads the paragraph aloud three times. Every other line has the author's odd stop-start rhythm; this one has none, and the reason nags.
scene B: This author could break through with this book, but only if the voice survives. {N} staked a good deal on that voice at the acquisitions meeting.
scene R: The sentence is perfect and dead. {N} wants to scrawl a question mark across it big enough to tear the page.
scene G: The author writes the way people talk where they grew up, with the knots left in the wood. This one sentence has been planed smooth.
- W | flag it with a gentle margin note, and leave the choice to the author | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 75
- U | rewrite it three ways, each closer to the author's voice, and send them all | v: achievement | chance: 75
- B | ring the author and argue that the rough version will make both your names | v: achievement, power | chance: 72
- R | cut the sentence outright and leave a blunt note asking for the real one | v: self-direction | chance: 72
- G | let it stand; every book has one sentence written in another mood | v: tradition (acceptance) | chance: 72
- W>R | write the author's quirks into the style sheet, so no copyeditor irons them out | v: self-direction | chance: 92
- U>G | read the author's early stories to find where the voice first came from | v: universalism (understanding) | door | chance: 90
- B>W | smooth it and every line like it, and hand the author a tidier book | v: power, conformity | closed: approval: changing an author's words without asking; backfire: the author finds out at proof stage | chance: 75
- R>U | stay up with the manuscript until you can hear what the sentence should be | v: achievement | self_control: + | chance: 70
- G>B | take the author to lunch and let them come round to cutting it themselves | v: power | chance: 58
works: The author reads the note, laughs, and sends back a sentence only they could have written.
works: The paragraph sounds like a person again, and the edit goes back a day early.
fails: The author takes the note badly, and the next email is short and cold.
fails: The sentence stays as it was, and a reviewer quotes it as the book's one false note.

== three books due in the same fortnight
tier: everyday
at: employer
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 21-75
calls: WUG
holds: book editor
tone: trouble
life: work
horizon: week
roles: boss, colleague, partner
earth: three manuscripts are due back at the printers in the same fortnight, and there is honestly time for two
times: Most book editors, about once a year in the busy run before a season's list (estimate)
gap: 0.8-1
scene: Three manuscripts are due at the printers in the same fortnight, and {N} has honestly got time for two.
scene W: Every author was promised a date in writing, and {N} signed each of those letters.
scene U: {N} draws the fortnight as a grid on the back of a proof page, hour by hour, looking for hidden slack.
scene B: One of the three is the house's big autumn title. The other two are small books nobody upstairs will miss if they slip.
scene R: The stack of pages on {Ns} desk is taller than the coffee mug, and {N} is three days behind and furious about it.
scene G: {partner} has stopped asking when {N} will be home. The deadline season comes every autumn, like the falling leaves.
- W | work every evening until all three are done, as promised | v: conformity, achievement | self_control: + | mark: kept your word | chance: 55
- U | draw up a checklist so each book gets exactly the passes it needs, no more | v: achievement | chance: 78
- B | put the big title first and let the two small ones slip a month | v: achievement, power | mark: broke your word | chance: 92
- R | take the smallest book to a café and blitz it in one long, wild day | v: stimulation, achievement | chance: 78
- G | go home at a sane hour, and trust the books to keep till morning | v: security, benevolence | chance: 55
- W>U | ask for the standard extension the house allows, so every book gets a proper edit | v: achievement | chance: 60
- U>B | plan the fortnight so the big autumn title gets your best hours | v: achievement, power | chance: 85
- B>R | hand the smallest book to a freelance friend and keep your weekends | v: hedonism | chance: 85
- R>G | bring the proofs home and edit at the kitchen table, among the family | v: benevolence | chance: 85
- G>W | ring each author in turn and agree a new date together | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 80
works: All three books go to the printer, and the authors never learn how close it was.
works: The fortnight bends without breaking, and {N} gets a Sunday back.
fails: One book goes out with a typo on its first page, and the author spots it before anyone else.
fails: The schedule slips across the board, and {boss} wants a word.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- cleaner (T2-027): calls URG (cycle s4) / calls WB (cycle s2)

== the last two people in the building
tier: everyday
at: employer
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-75
calls: URG
holds: cleaner
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: moment
roles: an office worker, boss
earth: late at night the office is empty except for you, cleaning, and one exhausted worker still at a desk
times: Most night office cleaners, many times a year; about 1 in 50 US workers cleans for a living (estimate from BLS)
gap: 0-0.5
scene: The building is empty except for {N}, with the trolley, and one exhausted worker hunched at a desk on the fourth floor. Each knows a side of the place the managers rarely see.
scene W: The rota says the fourth floor is done by ten, desks wiped and bins emptied, whoever is still sitting there. {N} keeps to the rota.
scene U: {N} can read an office by its bins: the energy drinks, the shredded drafts, takeaway boxes at the same desk every night this month.
scene B: The worker at the desk looks like someone who matters here, and {N} has been hoping for a daytime contract in this building.
scene R: It is nearly eleven and the young worker looks ready to cry. {N} wants to switch off the screen and send them home.
scene G: Two people awake in a sleeping building, each doing the night's work. {N} pushes the trolley slowly, so as not to break the quiet.
- W | clean around the desk quietly and leave that bin for last | v: conformity, benevolence | chance: 90
- U | ask what keeps them at the desk so late every night this month | v: benevolence | chance: 72
- B | mention you are after more hours, in case the worker knows who hires cleaners | v: achievement, security | door | chance: 25
- R | make two teas and sit down with them for ten minutes | v: benevolence, hedonism | chance: 93
- G | say nothing and let them be; some nights are like that | v: tradition (acceptance) | chance: 92
- W>G | leave a clean mug and a biscuit on the desk, as you would for anyone | v: benevolence | chance: 90
- U>W | tell them about the overtime rule the managers hope nobody reads | v: universalism (fairness) | chance: 65
- B>U | glance through the papers on the desk while they step out | v: power | habit | closed: approval: reading what is not meant for a cleaner; backfire: they walk back in | chance: 92
- R>B | grumble together about the managers until you have a friend on the inside | v: power, benevolence | mark: made a friend | chance: 78
- G>R | hum along to the radio on the empty floor, as if the building were your own | v: hedonism | chance: 95
works: The worker looks up, and for a few minutes the fourth floor feels less empty for both of them.
works: The floor is done by ten, and on the way out the worker asks {Ns} name for the first time.
fails: The worker snaps that they are busy, and {N} pushes the trolley on with a hot face.
fails: A manager comes up in the lift and asks why the fourth floor is not finished.

== a client asks to cut out the agency
tier: everyday
at: employer
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-75
calls: WB
holds: cleaner
tone: mixed
life: work, money
horizon: months
roles: boss, a client family
earth: a family you clean for through an agency offers to pay you directly, at more per hour, if the agency is left out
times: Many agency cleaners in homes, once every few years; cash-in-hand offers are common in domestic cleaning (estimate)
gap: 2-4
scene: The family {N} cleans for on Tuesdays wants to cut out the agency: more money for {N}, less for the agency, and nobody the wiser.
scene W: {N} signed an agency contract that says clients cannot be taken on privately for a year. The family's offer sits uneasily next to it.
scene U: {N} works out the numbers on the back of a shopping list: the agency takes nearly a third, and four regular clients might make the same offer.
scene B: Four good clients, a car boot full of kit, a few cards in the post office window: {N} can see a small business taking shape.
scene R: {N} is sick of the agency's texts at six in the morning and rotas changed without asking. This offer feels like a door swinging open.
scene G: The family has had {N} in the house for three years, and the children leave drawings on the fridge for {N}. It feels more like kin than a client.
- W | explain to the family that the agency's contract rules it out for a year | v: conformity | mark: kept your word | chance: 75
- U | read the small print and find out exactly what the contract allows | v: security, self-direction | chance: 95
- B | print cards, buy your own kit and start a client list of your own | v: achievement, self-direction | identity | self_control: + | chance: 65
- R | say yes on the spot; the agency's six o'clock texts can go to someone else | v: self-direction | mark: broke your word | closed: approval: breaking the agency's no-poaching clause; backfire: the agency holds back the last month's pay | chance: 88
- G | stay with the agency, and keep Tuesdays as they have always been | v: security, tradition | chance: 78
- W>B | report the family's offer to the agency, and claim the finder's bonus | v: power, conformity | closed: approval: reporting a client family; backfire: the family finds out and ends the work | chance: 60
- U>R | work out how many clients it takes to go it alone, and set a date | v: self-direction | chance: 72
- B>G | take the cash in hand and put the extra towards the family holiday | v: benevolence | closed: law: undeclared cash income; backfire: a letter from the tax office | chance: 90
- R>W | get the other cleaners together and go to the agency as one about its cut | v: universalism | mark: defied an authority | chance: 35
- G>U | ask an older cleaner who went independent how it really went | v: self-direction | door | chance: 80
works: By spring the work is on a footing {N} chose, and the money stretches further than before.
works: The family is pleased, the arrangement holds, and Tuesdays go on as they always have.
fails: A letter from the agency arrives, quoting clause eleven of the contract.
fails: Two clients drop away, and the month's money comes up short.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- postal worker (T2-029): calls UG (cycle s3) / calls WBR (cycle s4)

== the gate where nobody waits
tier: everyday
at: employer
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 17-67
calls: UG
holds: postal worker
tone: trouble
life: work, community
horizon: moment
roles: boss, a customer on the round
earth: the person who always waits at the gate for the post has not appeared for several days
times: Most postal workers on a long round, about once a year; posties are often the first to notice an older customer is unwell (estimate)
gap: 0.8-1
scene: The person who always waits at the gate on {Ns} round has not appeared for several days. Familiarity has become a small responsibility.
scene W: There is a form at the depot for a customer who may need help, and {N} has never once filled it in. Today might be the day.
scene U: Four days of letters on the mat, the curtains half drawn at the same angle, the milk not taken in. {N} adds it up on the way down the path.
scene B: {N} has eighty more doors before one o'clock and a handheld timing the round. Stopping costs minutes {N} does not have.
scene R: {N} misses the wave and the same bad joke about the weather. The empty gate makes {Ns} chest tight.
scene G: Six years of the same face at the same gate, as regular as the seasons on the round. A gap in the pattern says something is wrong.
- W | report a welfare concern at the depot, as the guidance says | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 65
- U | ask the neighbours when anyone last saw them come or go | v: benevolence | chance: 72
- B | keep to the round; the job is the post, not checking on people | v: security, achievement | chance: 95
- R | knock hard and call their name through the letterbox until something stirs | v: benevolence | body: light | chance: 55
- G | leave a note in your own hand, asking them to wave tomorrow | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 40
- W>R | ask the supervisor for ten minutes off the round, and go back now | v: benevolence | chance: 65
- U>G | find a relative's name on old parcels and track the family down | v: benevolence | closed: approval: using what the post shows; backfire: a complaint about privacy | chance: 50
- B>W | make the council take it on today, and get the officer's name in writing | v: security, power | chance: 60
- R>U | climb the side gate and look through the kitchen window | v: benevolence | body: light | closed: law: going onto private property; backfire: a neighbour calls the police | chance: 75
- G>B | ask the woman at the corner shop to look in, and keep your round on time | v: security | chance: 65
works: They are found in time after a fall in the kitchen, and by spring they are back at the gate.
works: {N} learns they are only away at a daughter's, and the post is held at the depot that afternoon.
fails: Nobody answers, nobody knows, and the post goes on piling up behind the door.
fails: {N} falls behind on the round, and {boss} wants an explanation that a feeling does not satisfy.

== the round is redrawn
tier: everyday
at: employer
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 17-67
calls: WBR
holds: postal worker
tone: trouble
life: work, community
horizon: months
roles: boss, colleague
earth: the depot redraws the rounds, and yours gains eighty doors, a hill and a handheld that logs every stop
times: Most postal workers, every few years; rounds are revised as letters fall and parcels grow (estimate)
gap: 3-6
scene: The depot has redrawn the rounds. {Ns} gains eighty doors, a steep hill, and a handheld that logs every minute {N} stands still.
scene W: There are agreed limits on how long a round may take, signed years ago. {N} wonders whether anyone upstairs has read them lately.
scene U: {N} spreads the new map on the kitchen table and starts working out a better order for the eighty extra doors.
scene B: The new round passes the business park, where someone with a van could pick up plenty of private parcel work.
scene R: Eighty more doors and a machine on the belt timing every breath. {N} feels the old pleasure of the round drain away in a day.
scene G: {N} has walked the old streets for nine years and knows where every dog sleeps. Half of them now go to a stranger.
- W | log the extra hours every day and take the figures to the union rep | v: conformity, security | chance: 40
- U | redraw the walk order yourself until the round fits the shift | v: achievement, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 80
- B | take the voluntary redundancy and put it into a courier van of your own | v: achievement, self-direction | binds | drops: postal worker | mark: took a wild risk | tech: car | chance: 55
- R | walk out of the briefing with the others when the new rounds are read | v: self-direction | mark: defied an authority | closed: approval: an unofficial walkout; backfire: a written warning | chance: 25
- G | ask to keep the streets you know, even if it means the earlier shift | v: tradition, security | chance: 50
- W>G | write down which old customers need care, for whoever takes your streets | v: benevolence | chance: 95
- U>W | time the new rounds with a stopwatch and show they break the agreement | v: universalism (fairness) | chance: 45
- B>U | buy the depot manager a pint and find out what the next shake-up holds | v: power | chance: 75
- R>B | attack the new round flat out, and claim every minute of overtime it takes | v: achievement, power | chance: 68
- G>R | take the new hill as a gift: the best view on the round, every morning | v: stimulation, hedonism | chance: 88
works: Within a month the new round has a rhythm, and {N} is home by two most days.
works: The depot gives way on part of it, and the worst of the hill goes to a van round.
fails: The new round runs late every day, and the handheld marks {N} as the slowest on the shift.
fails: A letter about performance turns up in {Ns} pigeonhole, and {colleague} gets the same one.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- merchant seafarer (T2-032): calls WR (cycle s1) / calls UBG (cycle s2)

== a message from home on the night watch
tier: everyday
at: employer
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 16-65
calls: WR
holds: merchant seafarer
tone: trouble
life: work, family
horizon: moment
roles: parent, sibling, boss
earth: a message from home reaches you on watch at sea: you can reply, but you cannot be there
times: Most merchant seafarers, a few times in a career, being away for months at a time; there are about 1.9 million seafarers (BIMCO and ICS 2021)
gap: 2-5
scene: A message from home arrives during {Ns} night watch: {parent} is in hospital after a fall. {N} can reply, but cannot be there.
scene W: The watch is {Ns} to keep until four, and the bridge cannot be left. {N} reads the message twice and keeps an eye on the radar.
scene U: {N} works it out: eleven days to the next port, a flight from there, two days more to get home. The arithmetic does not help.
scene B: The contract runs four more months, and breaking it costs the bonus and perhaps the next berth. {N} has worked too hard for this one.
scene R: {N} wants to be at the bedside now, and instead there are four hours of dark water and a blinking screen.
scene G: The ship moves on through the night, and far behind it {parent} lies in a hospital bed. Sea and home have always pulled at {N} like two tides.
- W | finish the watch properly, then write a long reply at the end of it | v: conformity, benevolence | self_control: + | chance: 88
- U | find out from the hospital exactly how serious it is before deciding anything | v: security | chance: 60
- B | decide the contract comes first: the money keeps the whole family going | v: security, achievement | self_control: + | chance: 88
- R | record a voice message on the bridge wing, saying everything you feel | v: benevolence | chance: 90
- G | write back calmly that you will be home when the voyage ends | v: tradition | self_control: + | chance: 83
- W>U | ask the captain for a satellite call, as the rules allow in a family emergency | v: security | chance: 78
- U>B | work out the cheapest way home that still keeps your contract bonus | v: achievement, security | chance: 45
- B>R | offer the captain extra watches in return for signing off at the next port | v: benevolence, self-direction | chance: 45
- R>G | ring home and sing down the line the song your parent always sang | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 75
- G>W | ask your brother or sister to be there in your place, and keep your post | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 70
works: The reply reaches home before morning, and {parent} has it read aloud on the ward.
works: The news from the hospital is better by the end of the watch, and {N} sleeps for once.
fails: The signal drops, and the message sits unsent until the ship clears the weather.
fails: {sibling} writes back, short and hurt: everyone else is there.

== an offer of work ashore
tier: everyday
at: employer
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 16-65
calls: UBG
holds: merchant seafarer
tone: mixed
life: work, family
horizon: months
roles: partner, boss, colleague
earth: after years at sea the company offers you a desk in the port office: home every night, a third less pay
times: Many long-serving seafarers, once or twice in a career; port office jobs are the usual step off the ships (estimate)
gap: 6-12
scene: The company offers {N} a job ashore in the port office: home every night, a third less money, and no more horizon.
scene W: {N} has a crew who count on {N} at sea and a family who count on {N} at home, and only one of them can have {N} every day.
scene U: {N} lays out the two lives on paper: the pay, the pension, the months away, the hours in traffic. The columns come out almost even.
scene B: Ashore, {N} would be the one assigning crews instead of waiting to be assigned. The desk could lead somewhere.
scene R: {N} has never once been bored at sea. An office with a window onto the docks sounds like a cage with a view.
scene G: {partner} has planted a garden that {N} has seen in only two of the last six springs.
- W | ask the crew and the family what they need, and go with the greater need | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 60
- U | ask for six months ashore on trial before giving up the sea for good | v: security, self-direction | door | chance: 35
- B | take the desk and aim to run the crewing office within two years | v: achievement, power | drops: merchant seafarer | title: office clerk | chance: 40
- R | turn it down without a second thought; the sea is where you belong | v: stimulation, self-direction | identity | chance: 92
- G | take the shore job and be there for every season at home at last | v: benevolence, tradition | mark: came home | drops: merchant seafarer | chance: 75
- W>B | stay at sea and sign up for the officer's course that raises your pay | v: achievement | door | binds | grants: rank | chance: 55
- U>R | sign with a company that runs shorter rotations, two months on and two off | v: self-direction | binds | chance: 55
- B>G | use the offer to bargain for shorter voyages and more leave at home | v: benevolence, power | chance: 50
- R>W | tell the captain straight out that you stay as long as the crew does | v: benevolence | binds | chance: 78
- G>U | wait one more voyage, and see how it feels to come home this time | v: self-direction | chance: 85
works: The choice settles, and {N} sleeps well the first night after making it.
works: {partner} hears the news and laughs out loud down the phone.
fails: The decision is made, and for months {N} keeps glancing towards the water.
fails: The company takes the offer back while {N} is still making up {Ns} mind.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- professional beekeeper (T2-035): calls BG (cycle s3) / calls WUR (cycle s4)

== the colonies and the calendar disagree
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-85
calls: BG
holds: professional beekeeper
tone: mixed
life: work, nature
horizon: week
roles: a farmer, mentor
earth: the contract says the hives go to the orchard on Monday, but after a cold spring the colonies look far from ready
times: Most beekeepers who rent hives for pollination, in late springs every year or two; contracts fix the dates months ahead (estimate)
gap: 0.8-1
scene: The calendar says the hives go to the orchard on Monday. The colonies, slow and thin at the entrances after a cold spring, suggest otherwise.
scene W: {N} signed a contract with the orchard: forty hives on site by the first blossom. In this trade a beekeeper's word is the business.
scene U: {N} has kept records for nine springs: brood frames, temperatures, flights at the entrance. This year's numbers sit lower than any before.
scene B: The orchard pays well, and two other beekeepers would gladly take the contract next year if {N} lets the farmer down.
scene R: {N} has been itching for the blossom all winter. Waiting another week feels like holding a breath.
scene G: Bees keep their own calendar, and this spring it runs late. {N} watches the cold entrances and feels the season turning slowly.
- W | move them on Monday as contracted, and say nothing about how weak they are | v: conformity, security | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: hiding weak colonies from a client; backfire: the farmer lifts a lid | chance: 55
- U | open every hive, count the brood frames and decide hive by hive | v: achievement | chance: 85
- B | ring the farmer, explain, and agree a later date before a rival hears | v: security, power | chance: 78
- R | load the strongest twenty hives tonight and drive them out | v: achievement | body: heavy | tech: car | chance: 85
- G | wait the week the bees ask for, whatever the contract says | v: universalism (nature) | closed: approval: breaking the delivery date; backfire: the farmer holds back the payment | mark: broke your word | chance: 50
- W>R | draw up a loading rota so the move can happen the hour they are ready | v: achievement | chance: 80
- U>G | feed the weak colonies syrup and pollen, and watch how they answer | v: universalism (nature) | chance: 70
- B>W | buy twenty strong hives from a neighbour to keep your word to the orchard | v: security, achievement | mark: kept your word | chance: 80
- R>U | drive out to the orchard at dawn and look at the buds yourself | v: self-direction | tech: car | chance: 95
- G>B | ask the old beekeeper down the valley for strong colonies on credit | v: security, achievement | binds | chance: 60
works: The orchard blossoms with the hives humming in it, and the farmer books forty more for next spring.
works: The colonies build up within the week, and the move goes smoothly at dusk.
fails: Half the hives arrive weak, and the blossom passes with the bees barely flying.
fails: The farmer rings a rival on Tuesday, and next year's contract goes with that call.

== a third of the hives dead after winter
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-85
calls: WUR
holds: professional beekeeper
tone: trouble
life: work, nature
horizon: months
roles: mentor, partner
earth: on the first warm day of spring you open the hives and find a third of the colonies dead
times: Most beekeepers, every few years; US winter losses have often run 20 to 40 in 100 colonies (Bee Informed Partnership surveys)
gap: 2-4
scene: On the first warm day of the year {N} opens the hives and finds a third of the colonies dead, clustered on empty comb.
scene W: Losses this size are to be reported to the bee inspector, and the register has a form for it. {N} reaches for the folder.
scene U: {N} sends samples off and pulls the winter's records: mites, stores, the cold snap in February. One of them did this.
scene B: A third of the stock is gone, and a third of this year's honey money with it. {N} starts adding up what it takes to rebuild.
scene R: {N} sits on an upturned crate among the silent hives and lets the grief come out loud.
scene G: Every beekeeper loses colonies; the old ones speak of it like weather. {N} breathes in the smell of old comb and spring grass.
- W | report the losses to the bee inspector and follow the advice to the letter | v: conformity, security | chance: 75
- U | send samples to the lab, and find out what killed them before restocking | v: achievement, security | mark: learned a skill | chance: 78
- B | split the strong colonies hard and rebuild the numbers by midsummer | v: achievement | chance: 70
- R | drive to the queen breeder that morning and buy back everything you lost | v: self-direction | self_control: - | tech: car | chance: 85
- G | sell most of the hives and go back to keeping a few for love | v: security, hedonism | self_control: - | drops: professional beekeeper | chance: 72
- W>G | warn the other beekeepers in the valley, so their bees are spared | v: universalism, benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 92
- U>W | write up what you found for the beekeepers' association newsletter | v: universalism | identity | chance: 90
- B>U | pay a bee scientist for a day to go through the whole apiary | v: achievement | chance: 90
- R>B | borrow against the house for new stock: double the hives or lose the lot | v: achievement, power | binds | mark: took a wild risk | chance: 35
- G>R | let the survivors build up in their own time, and enjoy the slower year | v: hedonism, universalism (nature) | chance: 70
works: By midsummer the apiary is humming again, smaller but healthy.
works: The cause turns out to be clear and fixable, and next winter the hives come through.
fails: The rebuilt colonies dwindle again by autumn, and the honey money barely covers the sugar.
fails: The bank statement in June shows how deep the hole really is.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- funeral director (T2-036): calls WG (cycle s1) / calls UBR (cycle s2)

== one song for a divided family
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-75
calls: WG
holds: funeral director
tone: mixed
life: work, faith
horizon: week
roles: a grieving family, colleague
earth: a family arranging a funeral cannot agree on a single religious tradition, but everyone remembers the same song
times: Most funeral directors, many times a year; mixed-faith and no-faith families are now common at funerals (estimate)
gap: 0-0.5
scene: The family in the arrangement room cannot agree on a single religious tradition for the funeral, but every one of them remembers the same song.
scene W: There are proper forms for every faith, and {N} knows them all. The difficulty is that this family wants three of them at once.
scene U: {N} listens and maps it out: the widow's church, the son's mosque, the daughter's nothing-at-all, and one old song running through all of them.
scene B: Families choose the firm that gives them a service they can all bear, and {N} wants this to be the family that recommends the firm to the whole town.
scene R: When the song comes up the whole family starts humming it at once, then crying, and {N} feels a lump in {Ns} own throat.
scene G: Families break and mend around a death; {N} has seen it a hundred times. The song is the root they still share.
- W | lay out each tradition's rites and help them settle on an order all accept | v: conformity, tradition | chance: 55
- U | design a service in parts: a reading from each faith, joined by the song | v: universalism, self-direction | chance: 78
- B | steer them firmly to the one plan that keeps the peace, and book it today | v: power, achievement | chance: 82
- R | suggest they all sing the song together at the end, unaccompanied | v: benevolence | chance: 90
- G | let the eldest decide for everyone, as families always used to | v: tradition | closed: approval: overruling the grieving children; backfire: a row at the graveside | chance: 35
- W>U | explain what the law requires and what is only custom, so they know their choices | v: self-direction, universalism | chance: 85
- U>B | find out which relative is paying, and shape the plan around them | v: power | chance: 85
- B>R | call in a favour to get a live musician who knows the song, at short notice | v: benevolence, hedonism | chance: 75
- R>G | tell the family plainly that they are all mourning the same person | v: benevolence | chance: 72
- G>W | let them talk it out over tea, however long it takes, then draw up the order | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 70
works: The service holds all of them, and when the song begins the whole room sings.
works: The family leaves the arrangement room with one plan and, for once, one voice.
fails: The son walks out of the meeting, and the plan has to be redrawn twice.
fails: On the day, half the family sits on one side of the room and will not look at the other half.

