scene W: {N} took an oath to serve the court, and the court has not changed; only the agency taking its cut in the middle is new.
scene U: {N} reads the agency's contract clause by clause and finds where the real losses hide: cancellations, waiting time, mileage.
scene B: Half the experienced interpreters are refusing the agency. Whoever signs now gets the pick of the bookings.
scene R: {N} is furious. Twelve years of qualifications and evenings of vocabulary lists, priced below a plumber's callout fee.
scene G: The people in the dock still need someone who speaks their language, whatever the agency pays. {N} thinks of the families waiting in the corridors.
