works: Within the year the courts fix the worst of the rates, and {N} is still in the courtroom.
fails: The agency sends {N} two hours away to a hearing that is cancelled on arrival, unpaid.
fails: The month's income comes in at half the old rate, and the bills do not wait.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- professional conservator (T2-045): calls RG (cycle s1) / calls WUB (cycle s2)

== the damage is part of the story
tier: everyday
at: employer
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 23-75
calls: RG
holds: professional conservator
tone: mixed
life: work, art
horizon: months
roles: boss, colleague
earth: a banner comes in for conservation, and its stains and tears are part of its history
times: Most object and textile conservators, a few times a year; whether to keep the marks of use is a core question of the trade (estimate)
gap: 0-0.5
scene: The banner on {Ns} bench is torn and stained from the day it was carried through the great strike. Removing every mark would also remove part of its story.
scene W: The conservation code is plain: do the least that is needed, and make every step reversible. The museum wants it looking new for the anniversary.
scene U: Under the magnifier {N} can tell the old stains apart: rain, soot, and one long rusty mark that might be something else entirely.
scene B: The anniversary show will be the museum's biggest in years, {boss} wants a banner that photographs well, and {Ns} contract is up for renewal.
scene R: {N} can almost hear the crowd it was carried through. Every tear in it seems to shout.
scene G: Cloth ages like everything else, and this cloth has aged with a town's memory in it. {N} wants to help it last, not make it young.
- W | follow the code: stabilise it, clean nothing that is history, record every step | v: conformity, tradition | chance: 90
- U | analyse each stain, and keep only the ones that belong to that day | v: achievement, universalism | mark: learned a skill | chance: 72
- B | give the director the bright banner they want; the renewal depends on it | v: security, achievement | chance: 90
- R | argue at the planning meeting for showing it torn, stained and as carried | v: self-direction, universalism | chance: 45
- G | do almost nothing: support the torn parts and let it hang as it has aged | v: tradition, universalism | identity | chance: 45
- W>U | photograph and record every mark before anything touches it | v: tradition | chance: 95
- U>B | write a report so learned that the director cannot argue with it | v: power | chance: 62
- B>R | go over the director's head to the trustees to show it as it is | v: self-direction | mark: defied an authority | chance: 45
- R>G | go and find the strikers' grandchildren, and ask what the marks mean to them | v: tradition, benevolence | door | chance: 75
- G>W | propose the middle way the old conservators used: clean the face, keep the scars | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 68
works: The banner goes on show, and visitors stand in front of it for a long time.
works: The director reads {Ns} plan, nods, and the work goes ahead as {N} chose.
fails: The director overrules {N}, and the banner goes on show in a way {N} did not choose.
fails: A small test clean lifts more than {N} meant, and the treatment record has to say so.

== the grant runs out in June
tier: everyday
at: employer
rate: 0.5
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 23-75
calls: WUB
holds: professional conservator
tone: trouble
life: work, money
horizon: months
roles: boss, colleague
earth: the grant that pays for your post runs out in June, and three hundred objects are still waiting
times: Many museum conservators, every few years; many posts are fixed-term and paid by project grants (estimate)
gap: 2-4
scene: The grant that pays for {Ns} post runs out in June. Three hundred objects are still on the waiting list, and the museum has no money to keep {N} on.
scene W: {N} promised the trustees a condition survey of the whole collection. It is two-thirds done.
scene U: {N} ranks the waiting objects by risk: which will crumble without care in five years, and which can wait twenty.
scene B: Private collectors pay three times what the museum does, and {N} has a list of their numbers from a conference.
scene R: {N} is furious at a board that found money for a new café and none for the collection.
scene G: Some of these objects have waited a hundred years. Some can wait another hundred, and some cannot.
- W | finish the survey before June, so whoever comes next can pick it up | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: kept your word | chance: 70
- U | rank the waiting objects by risk, and save the most fragile first | v: universalism, achievement | chance: 92
- B | ring the collectors and set up a private studio for when the grant ends | v: achievement, self-direction | binds | chance: 65
- R | tell the trustees to their faces what the new café cost the collection | v: universalism | mark: defied an authority | chance: 20
- G | accept that not everything can be saved, and pack the rest well for a long wait | v: tradition, universalism | chance: 78
- W>B | write the grant renewal properly, with every figure and letter of support | v: achievement, security | chance: 25
- U>R | research the objects' stories and launch a public appeal around them | v: self-direction | chance: 30
- B>G | pitch the collection to a rich local family who want their name on something lasting | v: achievement | chance: 25
- R>W | rally the volunteers to give their weekends to the packing | v: benevolence | chance: 82
- G>U | spend the last months teaching the volunteers everything you know | v: benevolence, universalism | chance: 80
works: By June the most fragile objects are safe, and the work has a future past {Ns} contract.
works: Money turns up from somewhere unexpected, and the post runs another three years.
fails: June comes, the lab is locked, and two hundred objects wait in the dark.
fails: {N} works every weekend to the end and still leaves with the survey unfinished.
